>I did a quick look-over the diff, and I noticed this: color == (color <
>NO_COLOR) ? C_GRAY : C_WHITE;
>It should be: color = [...];

As I type this, it is fixed.

>A yellow beehive filled with yellow bees is hard to figure out
>at a glance.

Why? I didn't remove the "You enter a giant beehive" message. It's just
acknowledging that the walls of the beehives are covered in honeycomb.
(Which is of no consequence to the player, owing to the dungeon trash
rule of NetHack)

>This presumably makes it much harder to distinguish ground from lava.

Why, might I ask? The ground is still '.' and the lava is still '}'.
It's no worse than playing in black-and-white (and a colour monitor is,
at least up to this point, not a requirement for playing NetHack.)

>It seems like brown, or even black, would be a more "realistic" colour
>for the rock surrounding lava

It's the Plane of Fire, so, um, I wanted it to be less
Earth-reminiscent. A vain hope, maybe?

>Shouldn't the floor on the plane of
> Earth also be coloured brown?

Interesting story, that. You see, if I made the Earth floor brown, I'd
have to make the corridors (created by digging) brown as well. And the
thing about the "litcorridor" option is that it changes the colour of
lit corridor glyphs to the brigher version of that colour. "Bright
gray" is white, and "bright brown" is, in fact, yellow. So it wouldn't
quite work out, you see?

>Perhaps the patch could "fix" this in the definitions of Grotto and
>Bustling town? Otherwise it's going to look odd, isn't it?

I suppose I must.

>I don't like this. Won't the black altars turn out blue for people
